SECTION 14 — NEARBY DISCOVERY ENGINE
===========================================================

Implement privacy-first proximity discovery.

TECHNOLOGY:

PostgreSQL + PostGIS.

Use geographic indexes and distance queries.

Consider geohash or H3 only if actual indexing, aggregation or scaling requirements justify it.

USER CONTROLS:

- Enable nearby discovery.
- Disable nearby discovery.
- Select discovery radius.
- Pause discovery.
- Block users.
- Control visibility.

Initial radius options:

- 100m.
- 200m.
- Custom permitted range.

BACKEND LOGIC:

1. Validate user authentication.
2. Verify discovery opt-in.
3. Verify location permission and freshness.
4. Apply privacy and block filters.
5. Query eligible users within permitted distance.
6. Apply visibility settings.
7. Return limited profile information.
8. Never return exact coordinates.

Use ST_DWithin with an appropriate geography representation and spatial index.

SECURITY:

- Never expose raw latitude/longitude of other users.
- Do not expose precise distances if that creates tracking risk.
- Use coarse distance labels.
- Limit query frequency.
- Prevent repeated coordinate probing.
- Avoid storing unnecessary location history.
- Apply retention policies.
- Provide opt-out.
- Require explicit consent.

WEB LIMITATION:

A web application cannot guarantee continuous background location discovery when closed.

Do not falsely promise background proximity functionality.

Document native mobile requirements separately.

